## 1. Introduction
Nitrophenols, which are persistent organic contaminants, are commonly found in various industrial effluents, released from pesticides, plasticizers, dyes, and pharmaceutical industries [1]. Among the three isomeric nitrophenols, 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) is considered the most detrimental as it has been linked various diseases, including blood disorders, organ damages, and impairment of the central nervous system [2]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop some efficient methods to treat 4-NP contamination in water. So far, a variety of methods, such as adsorption [3], photo degradation [4], microbial degradation [5], electrocoagulation [6], etc., have been investigated for the treatment of 4-NP contamination in water. However, low efficiency, high cost, and long running times are the main problems encountered in these methods [7]. In recent years, catalytic reduction of 4-NP to 4-aminophenol (4-AP) has attracted extensive attention of researchers, owing to not only the high efficiency and mild reaction conditions, but also the fact that 4-AP is less toxic and of great commercial value in industry, especially in pharmaceutical industry.
Noble metal catalysts [8–16] have been proved to be active for catalytic reduction of 4-NP. However, they are very expensive and rare, limiting their availability. Therefore, non-precious metals as alternative catalysts have received considerable attention with emphasis on economics, efficiency, and sustainable development. To date, various non-precious metal catalysts such as iron [17,18], cobalt [19–23], nickel [24–26], copper and their oxides [27–34] have been investigated for the reduction of 4-NP. Among them, copper has been considered an excellent candidate, owing to its cost effectiveness and wide range of potential applications. It has been reported that Cu species without support can display catalytic activity in 4-NP reduction [27–29]. For instance, metallic ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ exhibited moderate rate constant $k$ of $0.0953\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ [27], while hierarchical Cu nanoparticle-aggregated cage catalyst displayed evidently enhanced catalytic activity $(k=0.624\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ) [28], indicating that the morphology of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ NP can substantially affect the catalytic activity. A ternary nanocomposite $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O-C u-C u O}$ having diameters of $50{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ also showed a good catalytic activity of $k=1.52\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ [29]. However, the ease of aggregation owing to the strong interaction of the nanoparticles may deteriorate the catalytic property during the reaction process. Accordingly, dispersing metal or metal oxide particles on various supports is considered as an efficient approach for preventing the aggregation and improving the catalytic performance [29–32]. Among them, carbon material is one of the extensively studied supports. To further enhance the catalytic activity of carbon supported metal/metal oxide catalyst in 4-NP reduction, a strategy of doping N heteroatoms in carbon matrix has been widely investigated [2,19,20]. For instance, CoNCC catalyst showed a $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ value 6.7 times higher than ${\mathsf{C o-C C}}$ in 4-NP reduction, and the improved catalytic activity was ascribed to better electron donor properties arising from N doping in carbon support [2]. However, reports concerning the effect of N doping on the catalytic performance of Cu catalysts are quite limited. Sun et al. recently reported the synthesis of multi-crystalline N-doped $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0/\mathsf{C}$ foam catalysts from N-coordinated HKUST-1/cellulose and its application in 4-NP reduction [35]. It was found that the in-situ N doping and the foam morphology strategy effectively enhanced the catalytic activity and stability. Inspired by this encouraging result, it is necessary to find other simple and facile methods to prepare Cu based nitrogen-containing catalysts to achieve a further improved catalytic performance for 4-NP reduction.
In this work, a novel N-doping carbon (CN) encapsulated Cubased catalyst $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ was derived by a facile method from the pyrolysis of a bi-ligand MOFs material $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)}$ $({\mathsf{B D C}}={\mathsf{p}}$ -Phthalic acid, $\mathsf{B P Y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridyl). The N species in the catalyst totally originated from the N-containing ligand in MOF precursor. The catalyst $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and its counterpart $\mathsf{C u/C u_{x}}\mathrm{-}$ $0@C$ (derived from the pyrolysis of HKUST-1) were characterized by a series of techniques to elucidate the structure, composition, morphology, and electronic state of the Cu species. The catalytic performance of the two catalysts was evaluated by the reduction of 4-NP with $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . It was found that the catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ showed much higher catalytic activity in comparison with its counterpart $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ under mild conditions. The promising efficiency of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\ @\mathtt{C N}$ in the reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP provides an attracting potential catalyst in industrial applications for many other similar reaction systems.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and reagents
Analytical grade reagents, such as copper nitrate $\mathrm{(Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}$ - - $3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ ${>}99.5\%$ , sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4}$ , ${>}98\%$ ), 4-NP $(>99.5\%)$ , N,N-dimethyllformamide (DMF, ${>}99.5\%$ ), $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $(>99.0)\$ , commercial $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ $(>90.0)\$ and CuO $(>99.0)\$ powder samples, anhydrous methanol $(>99.5\%)$ , hydrochloric acid $(36.0\%$ ${\sim}38.0\%)$ and anhydrous ethanol $(>99.7\%)$ were purchased from Tianjin Kermel Chemical Co. Ltd. 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid ( $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , ${>}98\%$ ), $\mathfrak{p}$ -Phthalic acid $_{\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C}}}$ , ${>}99\%$ ), $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl (BPY, ${>}98\%$ ), and 5,5-Dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO, ${>}97\%$ ) were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd. All chemicals were used as purchased without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of $C u_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)$ and HKUST-1
$\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{BPY})$ was synthesized according to the following method reported elsewhere [36]. In a typical synthesis, copper nitrate $\cdot1.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ), $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ ( $1.5\ \mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ and BPY $\left(0.75\mathrm{\mmol}\right).$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixture containing equivalent volumes of DMF and methanol. The resultant solution was magnetically stirred at room temperature for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then solvothermally treated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After that, blue precipitates were collected by filtration and washed with DMF twice, and then dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ under vacuum to obtain $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)}$ . HKUST-1 was synthesized according to the procedure reported in our previous work [37].
## 2.3. Preparation of catalysts $C u/C u_{x}O@C N$ and $C u/C u_{x}O@C$
The as-prepared $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)}$ or HKUST-1 was placed in a quartz boat, and heated in a tubular furnace in Ar atmosphere at a flow rate of $100\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ . The sample was firstly heated from room temperature to $250~^{\circ}C$ at a ramping rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ , followed by a further heating to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a ramping rate of $2~^{\circ}C/$ min and maintained for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The obtained samples were designated as $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ or $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ , respectively.
## 2.4. Characterization
## 2.5. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
The catalytic reduction tests were carried out in a standard quartz cuvette with a $1~\mathrm{cm}$ path length. Sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4})$ was used as the reducing agent. In a typical run, $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, $0.05~\mathrm{\mL}$ of 4-NP solution $(5.0\ \mathrm{\mM})$ , and $0.5{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of fresh $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ were added into a quartz cuvette in sequence, followed by the addition of $12~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ catalyst $(0.04~\mathrm{mg/}$ mL, $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ ) to the mixture. The mixture was quickly subjected to UV–vis measurement in the range of $250\mathrm{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ (Carry 50) at a scan rate of $4800~\mathrm{{nm/min}}$ . Catalytic reduction experiments were performed at different temperatures to obtain reaction rate constants and kinetics.
Cycle testing was conducted to investigate the reusability of the catalyst. Since the amount of the catalyst charged into the reaction solution was too little to effectively recover, the catalyst was remained in the solution after the completion of reaction, while $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of fresh solution containing high concentrations of 4-NP $(12.5~\mathrm{mM})$ and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ $(5.0\mathrm{M})$ was directly added to the reaction solution for next run. The above procedure was repeated 6 times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
The XRD patterns of the MOF precursors and the composite catalysts $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ are presented in Fig. 1. One can see in Fig. 1a that the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{BPY})$ and HKUST-1 showed almost identical XRD peaks to the simulated ones, suggesting successful synthesis of the desired precursors. The XRD patterns of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ are displayed in Fig. 1b. Three intense peaks at $2\uptheta=43.2$ , 50.1, and $73.8^{\circ}$ corresponded to (1 1 1), (2 0 0), and (2 2 0) planes of metallic $\mathtt{C u}$ (JCPDS 04-0836). The peaks observed at $2\theta\:=\:35.4$ and $38.8^{\circ}$ were attributed to (002) and (111) planes of CuO (JCPDS, 45-0937), while those at 36.4, 42.4, and $61.3^{\circ}$ assigned to (111), (200), and (220) planes of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ (JCPDS 05-0667) [29]. It should be noticed that less $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ species was observed in $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ than in $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ , though both ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ and $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ were detected on them. The mean crystalline sizes of Cu NPs were calculated to be $36.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $62.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ respectively, based on Scherrer’s equation, indicating that N doping can facilitate the dispersion of Cu species. The Cu contents in $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ were determined to be $61.9~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $63.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ by ICP-AES analysis.
The SEM image of $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)}$ displayed a well-defined cuboid morphology with average size of $2\ensuremath{-}5~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S1a), while that of HKUST-1 exhibited a regular octahedral morphology with average size of $8\mathrm{-}10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S1b). When subjected to calcination at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in Ar atmosphere, the derived $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u/C u_{x}}\mathrm{-}$ $0@C$ retained the original morphology of their corresponding precursors, but the surface of the particles became rough (Fig. 2a and b).
The TEM image of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ showed the successful encapsulation of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0$ NPs with $20{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ into carbon matrix (Fig. 2c), the nanoparticles were well dispersed and no noticeable aggregation was observed, while that of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ exhibited poorly dispersed $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0$ NPs with a larger average particle size of $60-$ $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2f). These data are in good accordance with the XRD results. HRTEM image of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ (Fig. 2d) confirmed the formation of copper and its oxide NPs in carbon matrix. The distinct lattice fringes of 0.213, 0.182, and $0.130\ \mathrm{nm}$ are consistent with the (111), (200), and (220) lattice planes of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ , and that of $0.246\ \mathrm{nm}$ is consistent with the (111) lattice planes of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ . Meanwhile, the polycrystalline structures of $\mathtt{C u}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ were clearly observed from the pronounced rings in the selected-area electron diffraction pattern (SAED, Fig. 2e).
Raman spectroscopy of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ composites are presented in Fig. 3a. Two characteristic Raman peaks at 1338 and $1587~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed on both carbon composites, characterized as D band (defects and disorder) and G band (graphite), respectively. It has been reported that the intensity ratio of D band to G band $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ is proportional to the amount of defect sites in carbon matrix, that is, the ratio is inversely related to the degree of graphitization [2]. The catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ showed higher $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value (0.48) than $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ (0.40), indicating more defect sites in carbon matrix in $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ . The presence of more defect sites in carbon matrix facilitates the anchor and dispersion of active $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0$ NPs (supported by the NP size with $20-30~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in the TEM image, Fig. 2c), which may play a positive role in the catalytic reaction.
The XPS measurement was employed to investigate the elemental and bonding information on the surface of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ . As illustrated in Fig. S2a, various peaks corresponding to Cu 2p, C 1s, O 1s, and N 1s were revealed on $\mathsf{C u/C u_{x}}\textmd{.}$ $\mathsf{O}@{\mathsf{C N}}$ , whereas only Cu 2p, C 1s, and O 1s peaks on $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ · suggesting that N element was successfully doped into the composite catalyst upon pyrolysis of bi-ligand MOF $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{BPY})$ . The high resolution Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ XPS spectra shown in Fig. 3b illustrated three peaks assigned to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ $(932.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ $(933.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ (931.7 eV), respectively [29], on both $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ . The relative amounts of metallic Cu, $\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ obtained by fitting and integrating the peak areas were calculated to be 0.43: 0.25: 0.32 on the surface of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ , whereas those to be 0.64: 0.14: 0.22 on the surface of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ . For $\mathsf{C u/C u_{x}}\mathrm{-}$ $\mathsf{O}@{\mathsf{C N}}$ , though every Cu species detected by XRD technique was also detected by XPS method, there is a discrepancy in the amount of $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ between the surface (XPS result) and the bulk (XRD result), indicating a fact that Cu NPs are relatively small and therefore unstable and prone to be oxidized on the surface. The highresolution C 1s XPS spectra (Fig. 3c) showed that three major peaks of $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ (284.2 eV), CAN $(285.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (287.4 eV) were observed on $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ [2,38], while only two peaks of $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ $(284.2~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ $(287.4~\mathrm{eV})$ on $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ . The presence of $C-N$ bond at 285.9 eV confirms N-doping in carbon matrix. The high-resolution N 1s XPS spectrum (Fig. 3d) showed three peaks at 398.3, 400.5, and $401.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ on $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ , which correspond to pyridine N, pyrrole N, and graphitic N, respectively [2,14,20]. This signifies effective incorporation of N atoms into carbon matrix. The N content in $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ was measured to be $2.89\mathrm{wt\%}$ from XPS data, while $1.28~\mathrm{wt\%}$ from elemental analysis, suggesting the enrichment of N species on the surface of carbon matrix. The high resolution O 1s XPS spectra (Fig. S2b) exhibited four peaks, two peaks at\~532.1 and $533.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are generally attributed to the oxygen in the surface OH groups and adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ [39–41], the other two at 529.9 and $531.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to lattice oxygen in CuO and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ [41,42–45]. which further confirms the formation of mixed valence copper species in both $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ .
The $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ -TPR measurement was carried out to probe the distribution and reducibility of copper species and the results are depicted in Fig. 4a. The extremely small $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ consumption peak centered at $149^{\circ}\mathsf C$ on $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\ @\mathtt{C N}$ suggests that most ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions in $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}(-$ BPY) have been reduced to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ during pyrolysis, and only a very small portion of $\mathtt{C u}$ species exists as $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ . While for $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ , a dominant $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ consumption peak centered at $378^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and two small peaks at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $267^{\circ}C$ indicates that there are more $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ in $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ than in $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TPR results are in good accordance with the XRD results, both suggesting that N doping in carbon matrix may inhibit the oxidation of metal Cu. This may originate from the excessive valence electron possessed by N atoms. N doping generally increases the $\pi$ -electron density in carbon matrix [2,19], which may restrain the oxidation of metal Cu. Moreover, the single reduction peak on $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ also illustrated that Cu NPs with small size was well-distributed, which agrees well with the TEM results. In order to verify the source of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ consumption peaks, $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ and $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ were both treated with $0.8\mathrm{~M~}$ hydrochloric acid aqueous solution at room temperature for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ to remove $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ . The complete disappearance of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ consumption peaks after acid treatment (Fig. 4a) indicates that the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ consumption peaks on the two samples were totally caused by the reduction of $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ , and had nothing to do with the carbon matrix. Moreover, the small $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ consumption peak at $149^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ on $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ implies that the $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ species existing in this catalyst is quite easy to be reduced to metallic Cu.
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C}$ are presented in Fig. 4b, and their textural properties are summarized in Table 1. The two isotherms are of type IV with an evident H3 hysteresis loop at relative pressures of $p/p_{0}>0.4$ suggesting the presence of mesoporous structure. The BET surface area and total pore volume of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ were determined to be $147~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.25\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, much larger than 89 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.11\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ (Table 1). The result implies that N doping in carbon matrix favors the development of porosity, which is beneficial to the access of the reactant molecules to the active sites.
## 3.2. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
The reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP in the presence of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ has been widely studied over a bunch of metal catalysts [2,35,46– 48], and the reaction kinetics is generally monitored by Uv–Vis spectroscopy. An absorption peak appears at $315~\mathrm{{nm}}$ when only 4-NP exists in the solution, and it shifts to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon the addition of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ (Fig. S3), accompanying a color change of the solution from light yellow to bright yellow, due to the conversion of 4-NP to 4-NP ion [49] arising from the alkalinity of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ .
The reduction of 4-NP by $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ over $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}/\mathrm{{Cu}_{x}-}}$ $0@C$ was recorded by Uv–Vis spectra and the results are presented in Fig. 5a and b. It can be observed that nearly complete conversion of 4-NP was achieved in only $40\ s$ when $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ was employed as the catalyst (Fig. 5a), while the conversion of 4-NP reached $90\%$ after $210s$ over $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ (Fig. 5b). The result suggests that the prepared $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ catalyst exhibits superior catalytic activity to and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$
To investigate the reaction kinetics over the two prepared catalysts, a pseudo-first order kinetics model was employed, since a large excess $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ $(n_{\mathrm{NaBH4}};n_{\mathrm{4-NP}}~=~400)$ ensured the constant $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration during the reaction. That is,
$$
\ln(c_{0}/c_{t})=k_{a p p}t
$$
where $c_{0}$ and $c_{\mathrm{t}}$ refer to the concentration of 4-NP at the initial time and any reaction time $t$ , and $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ represents the apparent rate constant. Plotting $\ln(c_{0}/c_{\mathrm{t}})$ versus reaction time t produced two fitted straight lines (Fig. 5c). The ratios of $c_{0}$ to $c_{\mathrm{t}}$ were obtained from the relative intensity ratios of the respective absorbance $(A_{0}/A_{\mathrm{t}})$ at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The apparent rate constants, $k_{\mathrm{app}},$ in the presence of $\mathtt{C u}/$ $\mathtt{C u_{x}O@C N}$ and $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ were derived to be $0.126~s^{-1}$ and $0.011\ s^{-1}$ from the gradients of the two fitted straight lines. In other words, $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ exhibited a superior performance, with a rate constant nearly 11 times higher than $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ .
Control experiments were performed at room temperature in the absence of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . In this case, only 4-NP adsorption took place onto $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ . The slight decrease in 4-NP concentration (Fig. S4) indicates that the disappearance of 4-NP during the reaction can be attributed entirely to catalytic reduction, rather than adsorption on the catalysts.
The concentration of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ has serious effect on the reaction kinetics, since this reduction reaction is triggered by $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . To investigate the influence of the amount of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ on the reaction rate, different molar ratios of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ to 4-NP (100, 200, 300, and 400) were employed to trigger this reduction process. As indicated in Fig. 5d, the $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ varies significantly with the amount of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}},$ suggesting an important role of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration in increasing the rate of 4-NP reduction.
The effect of 4-NP concentration and catalyst loading on the reaction kinetics was also investigated. As seen in Fig. 6a, increasing 4-NP concentration with a constant catalyst loading resulted in a decrease in $k_{\mathrm{app}}.$ . This can be interpreted by the competitive adsorption of 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ based on Langmuir–Hinshelwood mechanism [12,13,46,47]. At high 4-NP concentration, most of the catalyst surface is occupied by 4-NP leaving less surface accessible to $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ and, therefore, the observed decrease in $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ . Fig. 6b showed that increasing catalyst loading with constant 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentrations led to an increase in catalytic efficiency, the $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ was calculated to be $0.006\ s^{-1}$ , $0.032\ s^{-1}$ , and $0.143\ s^{-1}$ when $3~\upmu\mathrm{g},12~\upmu\mathrm{g},$ and $48~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathsf{C u/C u_{x}O@C N}$ was charged into the reaction solution, respectively, indicating that more active sites were utilized by 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ , therefore, the reaction rate was accelerated.
The reduction of 4-NP over $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ was conducted at four different temperatures $(17-45^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ to obtain the apparent rate constant at each temperature (Fig. 7a). The experiments were carried out at $n_{\mathrm{NaBH}4};n_{4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}}=100$ , since the time for complete conversion of 4-NP was too fast to record as the temperature increased at $n_{\mathrm{NaBH}4}!n_{4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}}~=~400$ . Then, the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ and preexponential factor (A) were calculated using the Arrhenius Eq. (2) (Fig. 7b)
$$
\ln k_{a p p}=-{\frac{E_{a}}{R T}}+\ln A
$$
The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ over $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ $(55.6~\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) is lower than those of $\mathsf{A g@P t}/\$ sepiolite $(69.02\mathrm{\kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.\mathrm{}$ ) [16] and N-doped ${\sf C u}/{\sf C u x O}/{\sf C}$ foam catalyst $(73.6-106.9\ \mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\$ ) [35], but higher than those of meso-Co-150 $(51.3\mathrm{\kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ [22] and 3DCoOs (37. $4{-}47.1~\mathrm{{kJ}{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}.$ ) [23]. However, the $A$ value was found to be $4.54\times10^{7}\ s^{-1}$ , which is much higher than the reported value of $1.3\times10^{4}\ s^{-1}$ over Cu NP-aggregated cages [28], suggesting that $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ has more active sites, which may be responsible for its outstanding catalytic activity.
To reasonably compare the catalytic activity of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ with some other catalysts for 4-NP reduction reported in the literatures, the mass-normalized rate constants $(k_{\mathrm{nor}},\ k_{\mathrm{nor}}=\ k_{\mathrm{app}}/m_{\mathrm{c}},$ where $m_{\mathrm{c}}$ denotes the mass of the catalyst) of some catalysts reported recently were calculated and tabulated in Table 2. It can be seen that $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ displayed excellent catalytic performance, the $k_{\mathrm{nor}}$ of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ $(6.3\times10^{5}\mathrm{~min}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ is superior to those of varied assortment of catalysts, such as some noble metal based catalysts [8–11], $\mathsf{A u}_{53}\mathsf{P d}_{47}\mathsf{N P s}/\mathsf{G N s}$ , $\mathrm{Ni-CeO_{2}-x/P d}$ ， $\mathsf{P d}/\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ NSs, $\mathsf{P t}\mathrm{-La}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\mathrm{-}Z\Gamma\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , and non-noble metal based catalysts, Co-NC composite [20], Ni/graphene [25], $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{r G O}$ [26], including some Cu-containing catalysts prepared in other manners [28,32,34,35,50,51].
The reusability of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ was examined by using the catalyst in six consecutive cycles without regeneration (Fig. 8). The catalyst $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ showed relative stable catalytic performance in the first three cycles, $96\%$ of 4-NP was reduced at a reaction time of $90\:s$ in the third cycle. Even when the catalyst was re-used in the sixth cycle, a conversion of $91\%$ of 4-NP was achieved at $190\ s$ showing an excellent stability of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}.$ As for the slight decrease in 4-NP conversion in the six cycles, the fact that the catalyst was remained in the reaction solution and new reaction solution was repeatedly added into it may be responsible for that, since the accumulated concentration of 4-AP in the solution may hinder the adsorption of 4-NP on the surface of the catalyst. When the cycle testing was carried out under the condition where the amount of each substance was amplified by 8333 times (i.e., $100~\mathrm{mg~Cu/Cu_{x}O@C N}$ was charged). After six cycles, the used- ${\cdot}\mathrm{cu}/$ $\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}@\mathsf{C N}$ was collected and characterized by XRD, SEM, TEM, and XPS (Fig. S5). The XRD pattern, SEM and TEM images showed no observable change from the pristine one, while the XPS spectra exhibited more CuO on the surface, the discrepancy between XRD and XPS results may be attributed to the formation of CuO layer on the surface of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ because of its exposure to air.
## 3.3. Suggested mechanisms
The catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ exhibited superior catalytic performance in 4-NP reduction to $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ and some other catalysts reported recently. Based on the above characteristics, some properties of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ may be responsible for the superior catalytic performance, such as the larger specific surface area $\mathrm{\tilde{N}}_{2}$ adsorption), smaller NP size, and better $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0$ dispersion (XRD and TEM results). Since both ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ oxides were detected on the surface and in the bulk $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O}\ @\mathsf{C N}$ , it’s necessary to elucidate which one plays dominant role in the catalytic reduction reaction. Therefore, $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ treated by $0.8\mathrm{{M}}$ HCl solution, where $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ species was completely removed as demonstrated in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TPR (Fig. 4a), was employed as the catalyst, and charged into the reaction solution containing 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . The result depicted in Fig. S6a showed that a complete conversion of 4-NP was achieved at 141 s, much slower than $40s$ for $\sf C u/C u_{\mathrm{x}}O@C N$ (Fig. 5a). It should be noted that the amount of metallic ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ decreased in HCl treated $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ because of the dissolution of $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ species in dilute HCl solution, so the moderate drop in the catalytic activity may arise from either a decrease in metal $\mathtt{C u}$ or the removal of $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ species, as the $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0$ interface was sometimes regarded as the potential active sites for 4-NP reduction [32,35]. To make this question clear, $2.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ was firstly treated in $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ solution $5.3\mathrm{\mM}$ , $50\ \mathrm{\mL}$ ) at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , then $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension containing $12~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of prereduced catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ was added into the reaction solution containing 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . In this case, $100\%$ conversion of 4-NP was achieved within $27s$ (not shown for brevity), showing a better catalytic activity. This result can be explained by the pre-reduction of $\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}$ to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ by $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ treatment in advance [29] without a loss in metal Cu. To further confirm the role of metallic $\mathtt{C u}$ in 4-NP reduction, the catalyst $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ was treated with a solution containing $0.8\mathrm{~M~FeCl}_{3}$ and 0.8 M HCl at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the resultant sample was washed with deionized water and dried in oven. In this case, both metallic Cu and $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ were removed, only N-doped C matrix was left and used as the catalyst in 4-NP reduction under identical condition. The result (Fig. S6b) clearly showed that almost no 4-NP was converted even after $5\mathrm{min}$ . The above results demonstrated that ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ was the active sites for the reduction of 4-NP. Finally, it’s interesting to compare the catalytic performance of $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{x}}0@C$ with that of commercial $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ powder (Fig. S7). One can see that, at high $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration $(n_{\mathrm{NaBH}4};n_{4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}}=400)$ , commercial CuO powder showed a moderate catalytic activity with an induction time of $90\:s,$ , while commercial $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ powder displayed comparable catalytic activity to $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ (Fig. S7a). But at low $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration $(n_{\mathrm{NaBH}4};n_{4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NP}}=200)$ , evident induction time was observed on both commercial $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ powder (Fig. S7b). The induction period is reported to be related to a surface restructuring of the nanoparticles before the catalytic reaction starts [12]. The shorter induction time on commercial $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ powder in the presence of higher $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration confirms again that ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ acts as the active sites for the catalytic reduction of 4-NP.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a highly efficient catalyst nitrogen-doped porous carbon-encapsulated copper composite $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ for reduction of 4-nitrophenol has been synthesized via facile pyrolysis of biligand MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(B D C)_{2}(B P Y)}$ ( $({\mathsf{B D C}}={\mathsf{p}}$ -Phthalic acid, $\mathsf{B P Y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bi pyridyl)). The catalyst $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\ @\mathtt{C N}$ benefits from N doping in carbon matrix, displaying higher surface area, more defect sites, smaller and better dispersed metal Cu NPs. These characters endow $\mathtt{C u/}$ $\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ to display excellent catalytic activity with an apparent pseudo-first-order reaction rate constant of $0.126\ s^{-1}$ , 11 times greater than its counterpart $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C}$ $(0.011~{s^{-1}};$ . Detailed experiments show that the 4-nitrophenol reduction efficiency is highly dependent on $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ concentration, 4-nitrophenol concentration, catalyst loading, and reaction temperature. An activation energy of $55.6~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ and pre-exponential factor of $4.54\times10^{7}$ $s^{-1}$ obtained for $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{O@C N}$ exhibit its outstanding catalytic activity in 4-nitrophenol reduction. The facile preparation, excellent catalytic activity, and good reusability enable $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}0@\mathtt{C N}$ to be a potential candidate as a highly effective catalytic material for various environmental contaminants.
